  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tonga&#x2019;s economy hinges on remittances, agriculture, fisheries, and tourism, but its growth remains modest and vulnerable due to geographic isolation, frequent natural disasters, including the 2022 volcanic eruption. The recent economic situation shows steady recovery backed by infrastructure investment and remittances, though medium-term growth prospects remain subdued. Social challenges are related to poverty, disparities of living standards between urban and rural people, limited coverage of social protection, and high unemployment for youth and women. The Employment Relations Bill (ERB) was drafted in 2020 but its adoption has been pending. Tonga National Tripartite Consultation Committee (TNTCC) is not actively functioning but its meeting is convened on an ad-hoc basis.  

The Tonga National Workers Council (TNWC) affiliates 11 associations in 2025, including seven informal workers&#x2019; associations, Public Service Association (PSA), seafarers&#x2019; union, and doctors&#x2019; and nurses&#x2019; association. Tonga Handicraft Workers&apos; Association was registered in October 2024. The TNWC serves as the national workers&#x2019; body representing at tripartite policy discussions like the National Employment Policy (NEP) adopted in 2025. The TNWC has played a constructive role in organising and protecting informal workers and migrant workers, while lobbying the government to pass the draft ERB. However, they face challenges with no registration of workers&#x2019; association nor labour legislation to regulate the rights, benefits and working conditions of private sector workers. With the technical and financial support of the ILO, TNWC provided training to the leaders of the associations and also reached out to more informal workers, while operating Migrant Workers Helpdesk to provide services to migrant workers. TNWC is needed to continue to advocate on informal workers&#x2019; labour rights, strengthen the roles and functions of the associations, and collaborate with employers to pressure to pass the ERB.</narrative>
  </description>
